\par\intbl \cf1 When Herc pilots get together and talk about \'93the best,\'94 Kaesar\'92s name is always the first one mentioned. He grew up in the bunkers of Old Fort Knox, the son of a general. In the early days of the Cybrid conflicts, he became the youngest rookie pilot ever, based on his strength, intelligence, exceptional health and stamina. Determined, he also didn\'92t hesitate to use family influence to get past bureaucratic stumbling blocks. In the end, there was no doubt that Kaesar belonged in the cockpit of a Herc. He proved himself in campaign after campaign, receiving 21 major decorations before he was thirty. As a squad commander, some compare him to the great Tola, though unlike Tola, Kaesar resisted promotion to higher command. Just as a promotion seemed inevitable, some minor disciplinary black mark would always set him back. Reportedly, Kaesar used his father\'92s connections to know when a promotion was coming, and when he had to, stage another \'93setback\'94 for himself. Kaesar was killed in action at the battle of Cheyenne Mountain, last seen holding off five Cybrids single-handedly so that his squad could escape.
\par\intbl \par\pard \pard\intbl\tx1705\tx2695\tx3955\tx5395\tx6925 Kaesar\'92s family used their considerable political clout to keep his genes out of the BGM pool. It took special mandates from the Main Synthetic Intelligence and the UNItech Militiacorps to force the family to release his DNA for Project: Atomic Man, and even then it was under the condition that protein blocks be incorporated to prevent duplication. This became a moot point when it was discovered that Endoplasm does not guarantee consistent duplication. The Kaesar Bioderm has excellent command potential, and retains the exceptional mind and body of his template. He also retains nearly superhuman abilities in using conventional energy weapons and piloting a Herc, and learns at an astonishing rate.
